++⁺ : All P xs → All P ys → All P (xs ++ ys)
-- Base case: when m is zero, xs is the empty vector. The concatenation with ys is ys itself, so every element of xs ++ ys satisfies P by assumption on ys.
++⁺ {zero} ps qs = {!!}
-- Inductive case: suppose m = suc k and xs = x ∷ xs', with P x and every element of xs' satisfying P. The induction hypothesis for xs' (ys fixed) gives that every element of xs' ++ ys satisfies P. Since (x ∷ xs') ++ ys reduces to x ∷ (xs' ++ ys), the first element is x with P x, and the rest satisfy P, so every element of (x ∷ xs') ++ ys satisfies P.
++⁺ {suc k} ps qs = {!!}
